# Think D&D Prototype

## Source

Joel wants a new monorepo: `joelhooks/cloudflare-agent-dungeon`.

The shape: sandboxed/distributed agents play D&D. An OSE-style Referee agent shapes the game and has access to source material. Player agents have fog-of-war and only know what the Referee explicitly reveals. The campaign is ongoing, with persistent world arcs/modules. The first prototype should run on Cloudflare using Think, Wrangler, and real Cloudflare secrets. The audience should be able to pull up a live running game.

## Problem

The prior `atproto-agent-network` repo contains useful architecture and some RPG engine code, but this demo wants its own product/repo boundary. Keeping it separate prevents the atproto network research project from becoming overloaded with game/demo concerns.

The hard parts are not “LLM says fantasy words.” The hard parts are:

- durable agent identity and sessions
- agentic loop/harness without inventing everything from scratch
- fog-of-war and visibility boundaries
- source-grounded rules lookup
- event-sourced gameplay/provenance
- real-time monitor surface
- Cloudflare deployability
- demo reliability

## Outcome

A runnable Cloudflare-hosted prototype:

- one OSE-style Referee agent
- two player agents
- three rooms
- Referee-generated micro-module
- player-visible state strictly scoped by revealed information
- live monitor page with streaming gameplay
- source/rules lookup against JoelClaw docs/PDF Brain initially
- architecture that can later adopt AT Protocol-shaped records/firehose/trust more deeply

## Expanded campaign ambition

Joel wants the prototype to grow toward an autonomous ongoing OSE campaign, not just a room demo:

- starts at session 0 in a tavern
- players must find adventure and glory instead of being railroaded
- persistent campaign world/map
- major urban hubs, rural towns, wilderness, and adventure sites
- opposing factions with agendas and clocks
- world events that progress autonomously
- gods, legends, legendary monsters, and deep consistent lore
- notable/exceptional but inexperienced player characters
- player-created characters during session 0, not pregens
- OSE character creation starts with `3d6 down the line`, then each player may make one ability score swap
- Session 0 uses core classic OSE classes first: fighter, cleric, magic-user, thief, dwarf, elf, halfling
- player choices affect the world
- OSE-faithful skill, XP, level, inventory, magic item, supply, food, and death pressure
- a legitimate random dice service / ledger, not model vibes

## Requirements (R)

| ID | Requirement | Status |
|----|-------------|--------|
| R0 | Audience can open a live running game during the CascadiaJS demo | Core goal |
| R1 | Prototype runs on Cloudflare using Wrangler and real Cloudflare resources/secrets | Must-have |
| R2 | Referee and player agents use an agentic loop with prompts, skills, goals, tools, streaming, and persisted context | Must-have |
| R3 | Referee owns global truth and players only receive explicitly revealed information | Must-have |
| R4 | First playable loop is exactly scoped: 1 Referee, 2 players, 3 rooms | Must-have |
| R5 | Referee can consult Old School Essentials/source material through JoelClaw docs/PDF Brain first | Must-have |
| R6 | Gameplay emits typed events suitable for monitor UI, replay, audit, and later AT Protocol-shaped records | Must-have |
| R7 | Codebase is real monorepo software: Turborepo, strict TS, Effect where lifecycle/workflow complexity matters, and repo-local Effect source mirror | Must-have |
| R8 | Public demo does not leak private/copyrighted rules corpus text | Must-have |

## Domain language

Use OSE idioms for canonical code/domain language. **Referee** is the canonical supervising role. DM/GameMaster/GM are historical aliases only when referencing prior source or external wording.

Runtime/domain split:

- `Referee extends Agent` is the top-level runtime actor / Durable Object supervisor.
- A `Referee` instance is named by campaign ID.
- `Campaign` is the durable domain state aggregate inside Referee: rooms, hidden info, event log, dice ledger, party workspace, player projections, and monitor/public projection.
- Referee is the actor; Campaign is the state being run.
- `RefereeAgent[referee]` is a long-lived Think child per campaign, not a fresh per-run mind.
- `PlayerAgent[player-id]` is a long-lived Think child per campaign. It represents a player mind/personality, not the fictional character itself.
- `Character` is the in-world role a PlayerAgent is acting as. Player and Character are separate domain concepts.
- Keep player personality/private memory distinct from character knowledge from a gameplay perspective. Player style can shape choices; character knowledge must come only from Referee-revealed projections.
- The first prototype should model `Player` and `Character` as explicit separate records, even while each player controls exactly one character.
- `Player` owns agent/personality/private memory identity.
- `Character` owns in-world stats, inventory, position, and character knowledge.
- PlayerAgent action proposals should distinguish table-facing in-character speech from Referee-facing player intent. Rough rule: in-character to other players/the table; player intent to the Referee.
- Public monitor should show both lanes when safe and clearly labeled: table speech/action in fiction, plus concise player intent for Referee resolution. Secret/private intent stays out of the public monitor.
- PlayerAgent proposals may include explicit private intent. Other players and public monitor cannot see it.
- Players must be able to keep secrets broadly and not leak them by default.
- Deterministic `Referee` parent is the access-control boundary for private intent. It reads private intent first and passes only necessary adjudication context to `RefereeAgent`.
- `RefereeAgent` should not automatically receive every private player thought.
- Split player-private data into two lanes:
  - `refereeIntent`: private from other players/public, visible to the deterministic Referee for adjudication.
  - `playerSecretNote`: private player memory, not sent to Referee, RefereeAgent, other players, or public surfaces unless the same PlayerAgent explicitly chooses to reveal/use it.
- Application-level rule: only the same `PlayerAgent` can read its `playerSecretNote` lane. Operator/debug tooling may have infrastructure power, but the game API exposes no read path.
- `playerSecretNote` must not ride along with action proposals. It is a separate private self-memory write/read capability inside the same PlayerAgent.
- Initial action proposal lanes: `tableSpeech`, `declaredAction`, optional `refereeIntent`.
- Player private memory tool shape: `rememberSecret(note)` / player-local retrieval only. This lane is fully private, non-leaked, and critical to gameplay.
- A PlayerAgent may later act from a secret through `refereeIntent` without revealing the original secret note. Once the actual secret is revealed to Referee/table/public, it is no longer secret.
- `refereeIntent` is stored only in a private Referee audit lane, not in public events or player projections.
- Event visibility lanes: public/table-safe events, private Referee audit events, and PlayerAgent-private memory.
- Other PlayerAgents do not receive another player's raw action proposal before Referee resolution. They receive only Referee-committed table events.
- Slice-one turn shape: players express intent from their current projections, then Referee determines outcomes and commits table reality.
- PlayerAgent action contract is strict typed/schema-shaped output, not prose for Referee to parse. Initial shape: `submitAction({ tableSpeech?: string, declaredAction: string, refereeIntent?: string })`.
- Source/reference to inspect for this boundary: JoelClaw docs `Game Angry PDF` (`docId: game-angry-pdf-47a26de25095`).

## Selected early shape: Think-first supervised actors

| Part | Mechanism | Flag |
|------|-----------|:----:|
| S1 | Cloudflare Think agents provide the first agentic loop for Referee/player sessions: prompts, tools, streaming, persisted messages/context | ⚠️ |
| S2 | Referee parent agent owns canonical campaign/world state, hidden information, dice ledger, event log, shared party workspace, and player projections | ⚠️ |
| S3 | Lifecycle supervisor models Referee/player/game states with XState-style statecharts and/or Effect services | ⚠️ |
| S4 | Game state is event-sourced: Referee/global events, player-visible projection events, monitor/public events | ⚠️ |
| S5 | Fog-of-war is enforced by projection/tool boundaries, sandboxed/firewalled player agents, a shared revealed party workspace, and private per-player sandboxes, not prompt instructions | ⚠️ |
| S6 | Dice system is first-class: deterministic dice service / ledger under Referee control, not hidden model vibes | ⚠️ |
| S7 | JoelClaw docs API is the first rules/source lookup backend; later Cloudflare Vectorize + FTS migration remains open | |
| S8 | Monitor site subscribes to a real-time event stream and renders campaign/player/gameplay state | ⚠️ |
| S9 | AT Protocol-shaped records/lexicons guide IDs, event schemas, provenance, firehose semantics, and future federation | ⚠️ |

## Operating loop

This is a learning-driven prototype, not a bulldoze implementation run.

Loop:

1. **Research** a bounded Think/Cloudflare question from source.
2. **Report** the finding in plain language with source pointers and tradeoffs.
3. **Implement** the smallest useful slice only after the mechanism is understood.
4. **Capture** the learning back into Brain.

Joel is driving and learning. Agents should not silently race end-to-end through the build.

## First spike questions

| # | Question |
|---|----------|
| X1-Q1 | How much of the required Referee/player agentic loop does Cloudflare Think provide today? Answered in [[resources/cloudflare-think-agentic-loop-research]]. |
| X1-Q2 | How exactly should Think sub-agent communication work for this game loop: `chat()`, Agent Tools, direct RPC, or top-level DOs? |
| X1-Q3 | What is the smallest Think app that can run 1 Referee + 2 players on Cloudflare and stream events? |
| X1-Q4 | How should tools enforce fog-of-war: per-agent tools, per-turn envelopes, projections, or separate DOs? |
| X1-Q5 | How should JoelClaw docs API be called from Cloudflare without leaking source corpus text publicly? |
| X1-Q6 | Should the monitor be SvelteKit from the start, or a simpler Worker-served UI for the first run? |
| X1-Q7 | What is the smallest OSE-faithful session 0 character creation loop where PlayerAgents build their own characters before entering the tavern? |

